  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nRoot Cause\nThe ```GaugeManager._createGauge()``` function implements asymmetric validation between V2 and CL pool types. For V2 pools ```(_gaugeType == 0)```, it correctly validates pool legitimacy via ```IPairFactory(_factory).isPair(_pool)```, ensuring only factory-registered pools can receive gauges. However, for CL pools ```(_gaugeType == 1)```, the validation was removed (commented out due to code size constraints) and replaced with ```isPair = true```, unconditionally accepting any contract as a valid CL pool.\nThe commented-out check require(_pool_hyper == _pool_factory, 'wrong tokens') only validated token addresses match, not that the pool exists in ```ICLPoolFactory```. The current implementation performs token whitelist checks via ```TokenHandler.isWhitelisted()```, but this validates tokens themselves, not pool contract legitimacy. An attacker can deploy a fake pool contract returning whitelisted tokens from ```token0()/token1()``` while maintaining complete control over the pool's internal logic.\n## Impact\n Complete Protocol Emission Theft:\n\nAttacker deploys fake CL pool contract using whitelisted tokens (HYBR/rHYBR)\nCalls createGauge() to obtain official gauge for fake pool (bypasses all validation)\nAcquires veNFT voting power and votes 100% weight to attacker's gauge\nEach epoch, minted emissions flow to attacker-controlled gauge\nAttacker extracts funds by creating fake LP positions in their controlled pool contract\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nPrimary Fix - Add Factory Validation for CL Pools:\n```solidity\n// In GaugeManager._createGauge() at line ~172-174\nif (_gaugeType == 1) {\n    isPair = ICLPoolFactory(_factory).isPool(_pool);\n    require(isPair, \"!CL_POOL\");\n}\n```",
